.Dd September 5, 2026
.Dt PG_LASWELL_MCP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pg_laswell_mcp
.Nd intelligent migration for PostgreSQL, over MCP
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Ar conninfo
.Nm
.Fl -call Ar tool
.Op Fl -args Ar json
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
applies signed migration specifications to a PostgreSQL database.
It is not a generic migration runner: a specification states a business-level
.Em change intent ,
and
.Nm
reads the live catalog to decide how to apply it, based on what the target
table actually is rather than on what the statement says.
.Pp
Two properties motivate it.
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Em The safe method is a property of the table, not the statement .
.Ic CREATE INDEX
is instant on an empty table and an outage at a billion rows.
A script written elsewhere, against a database of a different size, cannot
know which it faces.
.It
.Em Pacing depends on contention, not on row count .
A backfill batched at a fixed number of rows per commit holds locks for
however long that many rows happens to take today.
It should instead commit when something starts waiting on it.
.El
.Pp
Measured on 300 000 rows with an application contending: 48 of 63 commits
fired on a lock waiter and none on the interval, finishing in the same wall
time as an uncontended run.
.Ss What intelligent means here
It is a claim with a specific meaning rather than a slogan.
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
It measures before it chooses, and every choice names the reading that decided
it, so the reasoning can be argued with rather than trusted.
.It
It paces on contention rather than on a schedule.
.It
It refuses what it can prove wrong, and says plainly where it can prove
nothing.
.It
It converges: one specification creates an index where it is missing and
corrects the name where somebody built it by hand.
.It
It never states a number it cannot derive.
.El
.Pp
Intelligence here is measurement, a refusal to guess, and an honest account of
the boundary between the two.
The judgement the tool does not have is supplied by the agent driving it.
.Ss Its sibling
.Xr pg_licht_mcp 1
is read-only by invariant and can only advise; this program is allowed to act.
Use both.
.Nm
measures the harm
.Em it
causes, meaning backends transitively blocked by its own backend.
It is blind to harm it inflicts without holding a lock: I/O bandwidth, WAL
volume, replication lag, and the vacuum debt a large backfill leaves behind.
Those are pg_licht's readings, and
.Nm
names them where they matter rather than guessing at them.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c , Fl -config Ar file
Connection registry, in INI format.
.It Fl -call Ar tool
Run one tool, write its JSON payload to standard output, and exit.
See
.Sx EXIT STATUS ,
which is the point of this mode.
.It Fl -args Ar json
Arguments for
.Fl -call .
A literal JSON object, or
.Cm @ Ns Ar file
to read one from a file, or
.Cm @-
to read one from standard input.
Defaults to
.Ql {} .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh OPERATIONS
Eight tools.
Four of them execute nothing.
.Ss checkPrivileges
Reports what the connecting role can actually do here: whether the ledger is
installed, whether this role can read the trust table and write the ledger, and
whether it can see other backends.
Worth calling first against an unfamiliar connection, because a
privilege-filtered answer is easy to mistake for an empty one.
.Pp
It distinguishes
.Em denied
from
.Em degraded ,
and the distinction matters: a role without
.Va pg_read_all_stats
cannot see other backends' query text, but
.Fn pg_blocking_pids
and the ungranted rows of
.Va pg_locks
are visible to any role.
Pacing therefore works, and the waiter is merely anonymous.
Reading that as
.Qq nobody is waiting
would be wrong.
.Ss getSpecDigest
Returns the exact canonical bytes to sign for a specification, and their
SHA-256 digest.
.Nm
verifies signatures and never creates them, so the private key stays outside
this process.
See
.Sx SIGNING .
.Ss validateSpec
Parses a specification and checks it against both trust gates.
Reports the ledger's state too, so
.Qq you have not bootstrapped
is distinguishable from
.Qq your signer is not trusted here .
.Ss planMigration
Measures the target tables and produces the ordered plan that would be applied,
saying which reading decided each choice.
Executes nothing.
.Pp
The plan names its transaction-group boundaries, which is where atomicity ends,
and carries a
.Va planDigest
that
.Ic startMigration
must reproduce.
That is how
.Qq what ran is what you were shown
becomes checkable rather than promised.
.Pp
By default it also performs a
.Sx DRY RUN .
.Ss listMigrations
Reads a directory of specifications and reports what is pending against this
database, in dependency order, with the groups that may run concurrently.
See
.Sx THE REPOSITORY .
.Ss startMigration
Plans and then applies.
Returns a job id immediately; the work runs in the background, because the
stdio loop must stay answerable while a migration runs.
.Ss jobStatus
Reports every migration this server is running: per-step progress, the
backfill's commit-reason histogram, and the contention it is causing.
Also lists jobs that died with their connection.
.Ss cancelJob
Asks a running migration to stop.
Cooperative: it stops at the next batch boundary after committing what it has
done, and the committed cursor lets a later job resume rather than start over.
.Sh THE REPOSITORY
A directory of signed specifications, classified against one database's ledger.
Note that pending is always relative to a target database: the ledger is per
database, so the same directory is at a different point on staging than on
production.
.Pp
Statuses are
.Cm pending ,
.Cm applied ,
.Cm in_progress ,
.Cm failed ,
.Cm unreadable ,
and
.Cm modified_after_apply .
.Pp
The last is the one worth having.
A specification whose id was applied under a
.Em different
digest was edited after the fact, so the database no longer matches the file
that claims to describe it.
The remedy is never to re-apply it but to write a new specification for the
difference; the bytes that were applied are kept in
.Va laswell.migration.canonical_bytes .
.Ss Order and concurrency
Order comes from
.Va depends_on ,
because business ordering is not derivable by any amount of measurement.
Concurrency is derived.
.Pp
The asymmetry at the heart of it, stated in the tool's own output:
.Nm
can prove that two migrations
.Em conflict ,
because they touch a shared relation, and cannot prove that they are
.Em independent .
.Pp
What it derives is the union of each specification's declared tables, the
relations PostgreSQL reports in the rendered SQL's plan
.Pq which catches tables hidden inside opaque expressions that no intent names ,
and foreign-key edges in both directions.
What it cannot derive is what a trigger function writes: the catalog names the
trigger and the function, and stops there.
A trigger function can write any table, so one trigger anywhere makes
concurrency unprovable, and the tool says so rather than guessing.
.Pp
Advisory locks refuse an unsafe overlap whatever the caller decides, so a wrong
call is refused rather than allowed to corrupt.
.Sh DRY RUN
.Ic planMigration
applies the whole plan in a transaction and rolls it back, so later steps are
checked against the schema the earlier ones produce.
Almost all PostgreSQL DDL is transactional, which is what makes this possible,
and nothing weaker works: a backfill referencing a column that step 0 adds does
not parse until step 0 has run, and that is the ordinary case.
.Pp
Three costs, all bounded and all reported:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
It takes the real locks, briefly, and rolls them back.
.Va lock_timeout
bounds the attempt, so planning can never queue behind anything.
.It
.Ic CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY
cannot run inside a transaction block, so it is listed as unverified rather
than silently passed.
.It
Statements are planned with
.Ic EXPLAIN Pq Cm GENERIC_PLAN ,
never executed, and
.Cm ANALYZE
is never used.
.El
.Pp
Pass
.Va dryRun
false to decline it.
.Sh PACING
A backfill walks the table by keyset and commits on whichever comes first:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm lock_waiter
somebody is blocked behind this transaction
.It Cm interval
.Va commit_interval_ms
has elapsed
.It Cm batch_cap
.Va batch_cap_rows
rows since the last commit, a backstop against pathological row widths
.El
.Pp
Two knobs that are easy to conflate and must not be.
.Va batch_rows
bounds how long
.Em one statement
runs, and therefore worst-case waiter latency.
The triggers above bound how long
.Em one transaction
runs, and therefore how long locks accumulate.
A fixed row count on a wide table can produce an arbitrarily long transaction.
.Pp
The lock-waiter trigger is an
.Em optimisation, not the mechanism .
Time-based commit alone already bounds waiter latency; the waiter trigger is
what lets the interval be raised, buying fewer commits and less WAL, without
raising worst-case latency.
.Ss The contention breaker
On the
.Em transitive
count of backends blocked by this job, because a direct-blocker count
understates a pile-up:
.Fn pg_blocking_pids
returns direct blockers only, and second-order waiters are invisible to it.
Measured, a direct count saw one waiter where the real pile-up was six.
.Pp
The ladder throttles before it pauses, deliberately: pausing a backfill that is
mostly done and mildly contending is worse than slowing it down, and inflicted
blocking grows quadratically with block duration, so reducing it continuously
beats stopping late.
Resume requires the transitive count to
.Em fall ,
never merely that this job committed: waiters can remain after a commit,
because the backend ahead of them in the queue has taken its lock.
.Sh SPECIFICATIONS
A specification is JSON, canonicalised with RFC 8785, and signed with Ed25519.
Reformatting the file does not invalidate the signature; changing any value
does.
.Pp
Top-level keys are an
.Em allowlist :
.Va laswell_spec_version ,
.Va id ,
.Va description ,
.Va rationale ,
.Va target ,
.Va depends_on ,
.Va intents ,
and the unsigned
.Va signatures .
An unknown key is refused rather than ignored, because a key one binary skips
and another honours is a silent difference between what was reviewed and what
ran.
.Pp
Floating-point values are refused anywhere in a specification.
Nothing a migration intent needs is a float, and refusing removes the one
genuinely error-prone clause of RFC 8785.
.Ss Intent kinds
The set of kinds a binary knows is exactly the set it implements: there is no
recognised-but-unsupported kind, and an unknown one refuses the whole
specification rather than skipping a step.
This build implements
.Cm add_column ,
.Cm backfill ,
.Cm create_index ,
.Cm drop_index ,
.Cm set_not_null ,
.Cm add_foreign_key ,
.Cm add_check_constraint ,
.Cm drop_constraint ,
.Cm alter_column_type ,
.Cm drop_column ,
.Cm replace_view ,
.Cm add_unique_constraint ,
.Cm add_primary_key ,
.Cm attach_partition ,
.Cm detach_partition ,
.Cm rename_table ,
.Cm rename_column ,
.Cm rename_constraint ,
.Cm create_table ,
.Cm drop_table ,
.Cm delete_rows ,
.Cm set_row_security ,
.Cm create_policy ,
.Cm drop_policy ,
.Cm set_trigger_state ,
.Cm grant ,
.Cm revoke ,
.Cm create_schema ,
.Cm drop_schema ,
.Cm create_extension ,
.Cm drop_extension ,
.Cm create_type ,
.Cm drop_type ,
.Cm add_enum_value ,
.Cm create_function ,
.Cm drop_function ,
.Cm create_trigger ,
.Cm drop_trigger ,
.Cm create_sequence ,
.Cm drop_sequence ,
.Cm drop_view ,
.Cm insert_rows ,
.Cm update_rows ,
.Cm merge_rows
and
.Cm copy_rows .
.Pp
An intent that creates an object must carry a
.Va comment ,
which becomes its
.Ic COMMENT ON .
.Pp
.Cm add_column
must state
.Va nullable
explicitly.
There is no default, because
.Ic NOT NULL
on an existing table is a different risk class from a nullable add.
.Pp
.Cm backfill
takes a single-column
.Va key
that must lead a unique index, a
.Va set
of column-to-expression mappings, an optional
.Va from ,
a mandatory
.Va where ,
and an optional
.Va verify_remaining
predicate that must select zero rows when the backfill is complete.
An unfiltered backfill is refused; write
.Ql true
to mean it.
.Pp
.Va assert_invariants
is a list of named scalar queries, evaluated on the worker connection before
the first batch and again after the last.
Both readings are recorded, and a difference fails the step naming the
invariant and both values.
This asks a different question from
.Va verify_remaining :
that one asks whether the work finished, this one asks whether the work broke
something it should not have.
A backfill can fill every row and still halve a total.
.Pp
.Va preserve
names a schema and table to capture the pre-image into, for a
.Em lossy
backfill -- one like
.Ql SET amount = amount * 1.19
where the previous value cannot be recovered from the new one.
A predicate-driven backfill needs none of it: its own
.Va where
clause is the record of what remains.
.Pp
The capture is a data-modifying CTE of the very statement that updates, so it
reads the rows as they were before that update and commits with it.
There is no window in which one landed and the other did not.
.Pp
The same option, with the same guarantee, is accepted by
.Cm update_rows ,
.Cm merge_rows
and
.Cm delete_rows :
a change saves the key and the columns it touches, and a delete saves the
whole row, because a pre-image of a deleted row that held only its key could
not be put back.
A
.Cm merge_rows
with
.Va when_not_matched_by_source
set to
.Ql delete
is the one form it does not cover, since the rows that branch removes are
exactly the ones the source cannot name.
.Pp
This is deliberately not a pinned snapshot.
A snapshot lets you look at the old values while holding back the xmin horizon
for the whole backfill, blocking vacuum throughout; this keeps them, durably,
and doubles as the revert path -- which a snapshot can never be.
.Pp
.Sy Naming convention .
Expressions in
.Va where ,
.Va set
and
.Va from
reference the target table
.Em by its own name ,
never by an alias:
.Ql orders.warehouse_id = w.id ,
not
.Ql t.warehouse_id = w.id .
Tables introduced by
.Va from
use whatever alias
.Va from
gives them.
.Pp
.Cm create_index
requires an explicit
.Va name ,
because a derived name is a name nobody can search for during an incident.
.Va on_equivalent_index
selects what happens when an equivalent index already exists under a different
name:
.Cm rename
.Pq the default ,
which renames it to the declared name so one specification converges every
database;
.Cm adopt ,
which accepts it where it is;
or
.Cm refuse .
A constraint-backed index is never renamed, because renaming it renames the
constraint too.
.Pp
.Cm drop_index
decides on contention rather than size: a drop is fast whatever else is true,
and what costs is acquiring the lock.
With nothing waiting it uses a plain
.Ic DROP INDEX ,
which is transactional; with waiters it uses
.Ic DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY ,
because a plain drop would queue behind them and everything arriving after it
would queue behind that.
A constraint-backed index is refused: PostgreSQL will not drop one directly.
.Pp
.Cm set_not_null
does not issue a bare
.Ic SET NOT NULL ,
which takes
.Va AccessExclusiveLock
and scans the whole table.
It emits four steps in four separate transactions: a
.Ic NOT VALID
check, a
.Ic VALIDATE
that does the scanning under
.Va ShareUpdateExclusiveLock
and therefore blocks neither reads nor writes, the
.Ic SET NOT NULL
itself
.Pq which now skips the scan, the validated check having proved it ,
and a drop of the now-redundant check.
Set
.Va keep_check
to keep that last one.
.Pp
The separate transactions are the whole point: if the
.Ic NOT VALID
add shared a transaction with the
.Ic VALIDATE ,
its stronger lock would be held across the scan and the recipe would buy
nothing.
.Pp
The temporary check is deliberately not named
.Ql <table>_<column>_not_null .
PostgreSQL 17 and later record NOT NULL constraints in
.Va pg_constraint
under exactly that name, so squatting on it would leave the permanent
constraint auto-suffixed for good, as a side effect of how the column was set
rather than of anything anyone asked for.
.Pp
.Cm add_foreign_key
splits the same way, for the same reason.
Note what the first step locks: adding a foreign key takes
.Va ShareRowExclusiveLock
on the child
.Em and
.Va RowShareLock
on the parent, so a statement naming one table locks two, and the table nobody
mentioned is often the busier one.
It warns when no index leads with the referencing column, because without one
every parent update or delete scans the child.
.Pp
.Cm add_check_constraint
takes the same two-step shape as a foreign key, for the same measured reason.
.Pp
.Cm create_index
on a
.Em partitioned
table is a recipe rather than a statement, because
.Ic CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY
is refused outright on a partitioned parent.
It builds concurrently on each partition, creates the parent index with
.Ic ON ONLY
.Pq ShareLock on a relation holding no data, so no scan ,
attaches each child, and then checks validity: the parent index stays
.Va indisvalid
false until the last attachment lands, and an index that exists, is invalid and
is never used would otherwise pass silently.
A unique index there is refused rather than planned, because PostgreSQL
requires it to include the partition key and this planner does not read the
partition key -- emitting a recipe whose final
.Ic ATTACH
would fail is worse than declining.
.Pp
A dry run cannot cover such a recipe: the concurrent builds cannot run inside a
transaction block, so the attaches that follow have nothing to attach.
Those steps are reported as
.Em unverified ,
never as wrong.
.Pp
.Cm drop_constraint
emits one statement whatever the database looks like -- there is no concurrent
variant of
.Ic ALTER TABLE ... DROP CONSTRAINT
and the lock is
.Va AccessExclusiveLock
in every case -- so by the rule that an intent kind must justify itself by its
planner's decision, it looks like a wrapper around
.Xr psql 1 .
It is not, for two reasons.
A repository has to be able to express the whole change: a constraint dropped
outside
.Nm
is unsigned, absent from the ledger and missing from the dependency graph, so
the repository then describes a database that does not exist.
And the decision is not which statement to emit but whether the drop can
succeed at all, what it takes with it, and how far the lock reaches.
.Pp
It refuses, before anything runs, a constraint another constraint depends on --
a unique constraint whose index backs a foreign key elsewhere.
PostgreSQL reports this at execution time and suggests
.Ic CASCADE ;
the dependency is visible in the catalog beforehand, through the shared
.Va conindid ,
so the plan names the dependent constraint and the table it sits on instead.
There is no
.Va cascade
key and there will not be one:
.Ic CASCADE
drops objects the specification never named, which is exactly what a signed
change must not do.
Drop the dependents in their own intents, where they are visible and
reviewable.
.Pp
It also says what goes with the constraint, rather than leaving it to be
discovered.
Dropping a
.Em foreign key
takes
.Va AccessExclusiveLock
on the referenced table as well, which the statement never names -- a stronger
lock on the parent than adding the constraint takes, and often the busier
table.
Dropping a unique or exclusion constraint drops its index, so every lookup that
index served becomes a scan; the plan names the index and points at pg_licht
.Cm evaluateIndex
with
.Va hide
for what still plans against it.
Dropping a NOT NULL constraint makes the column nullable again, which is a
change to what the data may contain and not merely to what is enforced.
A constraint that is already absent is
.Em satisfied ,
not an error.
.Ss Row-level DML
.Cm insert_rows ,
.Cm update_rows ,
.Cm delete_rows ,
.Cm merge_rows
and
.Cm copy_rows
change rows rather than schema.
They are migrations by the same two tests as everything else -- a change to
state, applied exactly once -- and a repository that cannot express its
reference data describes a database that does not exist.
.Ic TRUNCATE
is deliberately absent; see
.Sx What is not a migration .
.Pp
Every one of them names its rows exactly one way, and the choice decides the
pacing:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va values
Literal rows, in the specification, covered by its signature.
The count is known exactly at planning time, so the change runs in
.Em one transaction
below
.Va dml_single_txn_rows
and is paced above it.
A half-applied reference table is worse than one that failed and can be
retried.
Each row is an array positionally matching
.Va columns ;
cells are JSON scalars, and a
.Ic jsonb
or array value is written as a string in PostgreSQL's own input syntax.
.It Va select
A query producing the rows.
Its count is
.Em not derivable
-- the planner is a pure function with no database in it -- so these are
.Em always
paced rather than sized by a number the tool cannot measure.
.It Va where
A predicate over the target, for
.Cm delete_rows
and
.Cm backfill
only.
Paced, because
.Va reltuples
estimates the table and not the filter.
.El
.Pp
.Va paced
forces the decision either way and the plan says that it was forced.
.Pp
Every paced statement takes its cursor from the batch and never from the
mutation's own
.Ic RETURNING .
Measured on 18.6: a paced
.Ic MERGE
whose batch matched no target row returned zero rows, which the executor reads
as "the walk is finished" -- so the step would report success having merged
nothing.
The same trap waits for an update whose rows have gone and an insert whose
every row hits
.Ic ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING .
.Pp
.Cm insert_rows
takes
.Va on_conflict :
.Ql refuse
(the default, no clause),
.Ql skip
.Pq Ic DO NOTHING , which makes re-running the specification harmless
or
.Ql update
.Pq Ic DO UPDATE .
The last two need a
.Va conflict_target ,
because PostgreSQL has no default arbiter: measured on 18.6, an
.Ic ON CONFLICT
with no matching unique index is refused outright rather than falling back to
the primary key, and against a
.Em partial
unique index the arbiter must repeat the index predicate in
.Va conflict_where .
The planner refuses both cases beforehand and names the predicate to copy.
.Pp
It also refuses, before anything runs, the two column classes PostgreSQL will
not let a statement write: a
.Ic GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY
column without
.Va overriding
set to
.Ql system ,
and a
.Ic STORED
generated column at all.
.Pp
Where explicit values are supplied for a column that draws from a sequence, a
second step emits the
.Ic setval
that closes the gap.
This is the failure the kind exists to prevent: measured on 18.6, five rows
inserted with explicit ids into a
.Ic GENERATED BY DEFAULT
identity column left the sequence unset, and the application's very next
insert died on a duplicate key -- after the migration had reported success.
The value is read from the table at execution time, never from the
specification, so it stays correct when the seed was already partly applied,
and it writes nothing when the table is empty.
.Pp
.Cm update_rows
gives
.Em each row its own values ,
which is what separates it from
.Cm backfill :
a backfill applies one expression to many rows and that expression is the
record of what it did, while per-row values have no rule to re-derive them
from.
For that reason it warns when neither
.Va preserve
nor
.Va assert_invariants
is present.
Both it and
.Cm merge_rows
refuse a key with no unique index behind it.
.Pp
.Cm merge_rows
is the sharpest of these refusals.
.Ic MERGE Ns 's
.Ic ON
clause is not a key constraint and PostgreSQL does not pretend it is: measured
on 18.6, against a table with two rows sharing a key and no unique index,
.Em one
source row updated
.Em both
target rows, with no error and nothing in the row count to notice.
An insert would have hit the unique index; a merge has none to hit.
.Pp
Its branches are
.Va when_matched
.Pq Ql update , Ql delete No or Ql nothing ,
.Va when_not_matched
.Pq Ql insert No or Ql nothing
and
.Va when_not_matched_by_source
.Pq Ql nothing No or Ql delete .
The last one is refused when the statement would be paced: it means "delete
every target row the source does not mention", a paced merge sees only one
batch at a time, and the first batch would therefore delete everything outside
it -- including the rows later batches were going to match.
Unpaced it is allowed and warns that it is a full replacement of the table's
contents rather than an upsert.
.Pp
.Cm copy_rows
sends
.Ic COPY ... FROM STDIN .
It is the one kind here that is never paced and cannot be: measured on 18.6, a
.Ic COPY
whose second of three rows violated a constraint rolled back all three, so it
is a single transaction whatever its size.
It is not a fast path around constraints either -- foreign keys and row
triggers fire exactly as they do for an insert -- and the plan warns above
.Va dml_single_txn_rows
that
.Cm insert_rows
with the same rows
.Em is
paced.
.Pp
It accepts no
.Ic WITH
options, and refuses
.Va on_error ,
.Va reject_limit
and
.Va freeze
by name rather than ignoring them.
The reason is the client library, not a judgement about the features:
.Nm
records the statement that ran verbatim, libpqxx offers exactly one sanctioned
way to send
.Ic COPY
data, and it builds its own statement with no
.Ic WITH
clause and no way to add one.
A specification asking for
.Ic ON_ERROR ignore
and silently getting a copy that stops on the first bad row would be worse than
no copy at all.
.Pp
.Sy Version floor .
Everything in this family is measured against PostgreSQL 18, and
.Cm merge_rows
is the one kind that does not reach back as far as the rest.
Its paced form emits
.Ic MERGE ... RETURNING
and
.Va when_not_matched_by_source
emits
.Ic WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE ,
both of which arrived in PostgreSQL 17: measured, each is a syntax error on 15
and 16.
The planner refuses both below 17, naming the observed server version, rather
than emitting SQL the server cannot parse.
It refuses rather than quietly dropping to the unpaced form: that would turn a
bounded change into one long transaction holding its locks throughout, which is
the harm this tool exists to prevent, arrived at by a decision nobody made.
Set
.Va paced
to
.Ql false
and accept the transaction, or run against 17 or later.
The unpaced form works from 15, and every other kind in this family
.Pq including the paced insert, update, delete and backfill
runs clean on 15.
.Pp
Row-level security is warned about for all five, and the two halves fail
differently: a
.Ic WITH CHECK
policy refuses a row loudly, while a
.Ic USING
policy
.Em silently narrows
which rows are matched at all.
Measured on 18.6, a delete naming two ids removed one, reported
.Ql DELETE 1 ,
and said nothing about the other.
.Ss Dependent views
PostgreSQL refuses
.Ic ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE
and
.Ic DROP COLUMN
outright when a view reads the column, and it refuses them whatever the change
costs: measured on 18.6,
.Li varchar(50)
to
.Li varchar(100)
rewrites nothing and is still refused, then succeeds the moment the view is
dropped.
So the view stack is not a consequence of an expensive change; it is a
consequence of a view existing.
.Pp
.Nm
walks the dependency graph transitively, drops the affected views deepest
first, applies the change, and recreates them shallowest first, all in one
transaction so no reader ever finds them missing.
A view reading a
.Em different
column of the same table is left alone: it does not block the change, and
rebuilding it would widen the blast radius for nothing.
.Pp
The rebuild is the easy half.
A hand-written drop and recreate silently loses eight things, every one
measured: the view's comment, its per-column comments, its grants, its
.Em column-level
grants, its reloptions such as
.Va security_barrier ,
its
.Ic INSTEAD OF
triggers, a materialized view's indexes -- and with them
.Ic REFRESH ... CONCURRENTLY ,
which requires a unique one -- and its owner, which reverts to whoever ran the
migration.
Nothing errors; an application simply loses
.Ic SELECT .
.Nm
restores all eight, and restores the owner before the grants so each is
recorded with the right grantor.
.Pp
.Cm drop_column
is the one case that is refused rather than planned.
The recorded view definitions still select the column being removed, so
replaying them verbatim would fail, and rewriting a view body is a judgement
about what that view should now mean.
Change or drop those views in earlier intents, where the new definition is
written down and reviewable.
There is no
.Ic CASCADE :
measured on 18.6, a cascading drop under a three-level stack left
.Em zero
views standing.
.Ss Schemas, extensions, types, functions and sequences
These are what a repository needs to describe a database from nothing, and each
carries the same safeguards as the rest of the tool rather than being a
wrapper.
.Pp
.Em Every drop refuses when something depends on the object ,
naming the dependant PostgreSQL would have named, and none of them emits
.Ic CASCADE .
Dropping a non-empty schema is refused outright: cascading there is the widest
blast radius in the tool.
.Pp
.Cm add_enum_value
is the sharpest case.
Adding a label is
.Em irreversible
--
.Qq dropping an enum value is not implemented
-- so there is no revert for the step in this tool or by hand, and the plan
says so.
The new label also cannot be
.Em used
until the transaction that added it commits
.Pq Qq unsafe use of new value ,
so the step takes its own transaction; without that boundary a backfill later
in the same specification fails on the label it just added.
.Pp
.Cm create_function
uses
.Ic CREATE OR REPLACE ,
which keeps the comment, the grants, the owner and the function's OID -- so
dependent views survive.
It cannot change the return type, and the drop that can loses all four, so a
return-type change is
.Em refused
with what the alternative would cost rather than quietly becoming a drop.
A
.Ic SECURITY DEFINER
function is flagged: it runs with the owner's privileges and bypasses
row-level security.
.Pp
.Cm create_extension
refuses a name the server does not have available -- that is an infrastructure
prerequisite, not something a specification can fix -- and warns when the spec
names no schema, because an extension's objects land wherever
.Va search_path
points and moving them later is far harder than choosing now.
.Pp
.Cm create_trigger
takes
.Va ShareRowExclusiveLock ,
so it blocks writes and not reads, and warns that the trigger fires for rows
changed from the commit onwards and for none of the rows already there.
.Ss Renames
A rename repairs the catalog by itself.
Dependencies are held by OID, so after renaming a column the dependent view's
definition becomes
.Ql SELECT id, value AS amount FROM t
on its own, views stacked on it stay queryable, and every index, check and
foreign-key definition follows.
The lock is
.Va AccessExclusiveLock
on the table alone; no view is locked and nothing is rebuilt.
.Pp
What does
.Em not
change is the part people expect to.
The view keeps its own output column name, so after renaming
.Va amount
to
.Va value
the view still exposes a column called
.Va amount .
The rename therefore never reaches anything reading through a view, and nothing
errors to say so.
.Nm
names the views concerned and points at
.Cm replace_view .
Renaming a constraint also renames its backing index.
.Ss Row security, policies and privileges
These take one statement each and belong here by the second test above: each is
a change to schema state, applied once.
Their locks are measured --
.Ic DISABLE TRIGGER
takes
.Va ShareRowExclusiveLock
and so blocks writes but not reads,
.Ic ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY
and
.Ic CREATE POLICY
take
.Va AccessExclusiveLock ,
and
.Ic GRANT
takes only
.Va AccessShareLock
-- but the reason they are worth planning is that their effect is nearly
invisible from the session that makes it.
.Pp
Enabling row-level security with no policy is a
.Em default deny .
Measured on 18.6, an application role reading the table went from a thousand
rows to none, with no error: the query succeeds and returns nothing.
.Pp
And you will probably not be able to see it.
A table's owner bypasses row-level security unless
.Ic FORCE
is also set, and a
.Em superuser
bypasses it whatever either flag says.
Verifying an RLS change from the migrating session shows no change at all,
however wrong the policy is; check it as the application role, with
.Ic SET ROLE .
.Pp
The other quiet ones:
a policy created before row security is enabled is stored and does nothing;
dropping the last policy while row security stays on hides every row;
.Ic USING
without
.Ic WITH CHECK
lets a role write a row its own policy would not let it read back;
a revoke takes effect on already-connected sessions at their next statement;
and while a trigger is disabled, whatever it maintained is not maintained, with
nothing recording the gap and no backfill on re-enabling.
.Ss What is not a migration
An intent kind has to pass two tests, and the second one is easy to overlook.
It must make a real decision -- if the planner would emit one possible
statement whatever the database looked like, it is a wrapper around
.Xr psql 1 .
And it must be a change to schema
.Em state ,
applied exactly once.
.Pp
The ledger keys on the specification digest, and a digest that has succeeded is
never listed pending again.
So anything meant to run
.Em repeatedly
cannot be a migration here: the specification would run once, be marked
applied, and never run again.
.Pp
.Ic REINDEX
is the clearest example, and it passes the first test --
.Ic REINDEX CONCURRENTLY
against a plain rebuild is a genuine, bloat-driven decision.
It fails the second one completely.
It changes no state: the catalog is identical before and after, same index,
same definition, same name.
It can never be
.Em satisfied ,
because nothing in the catalog says an index has been reindexed.
And it answers a condition that is transient, so a signature attesting that
somebody reviewed it three months ago says nothing about whether that index is
bloated today.
.Pp
There is no gap to fill either.
.Xr reindexdb 1
ships with PostgreSQL and takes
.Fl -concurrently ,
and pg_licht
.Cm indexBloat
answers whether it is worth doing.
Use those.
The same reasoning applies to
.Ic VACUUM ,
.Ic ANALYZE
and
.Ic CLUSTER .
.Ss Partitions
.Ic ATTACH
validates the candidate against the new bounds unless a CHECK constraint
already proves it cannot violate them.
Measured on two million rows with the index build taken out of the comparison:
98.393ms without a matching CHECK against 0.914ms with one.
.Nm
therefore adds the CHECK
.Ic NOT VALID ,
validates it under
.Va ShareUpdateExclusiveLock
-- which does not block the application -- attaches, and then drops the CHECK,
since the partition bound now enforces the same thing and PostgreSQL would
otherwise evaluate both on every insert.
Bounds are given structurally, as
.Va from Ns / Ns Va to ,
.Va values ,
or
.Va default ,
because they must be rendered twice: once as
.Ic FOR VALUES
and once as that CHECK.
.Pp
Two costs the recipe cannot remove, both stated rather than discovered.
Any of the parent's partitioned indexes the candidate does not match is
.Em built
during the attach, under
.Va AccessExclusiveLock
on the candidate -- so build them first, and a matching index is attached to
the parent's rather than rebuilt.
And a
.Ic DEFAULT
partition is scanned on every attach, to prove it holds no row belonging to the
new bounds; no CHECK on the candidate avoids that, because the cost belongs to
the default.
Measured at 165ms with a two-million-row default present.
.Pp
A plain
.Ic DETACH
takes
.Va AccessExclusiveLock
on the
.Em parent
as well as the partition, blocking every query against every partition -- not
only the one leaving.
.Ic DETACH ... CONCURRENTLY
does not, and cannot run inside a transaction block, so it owns its own.
Its risk is stated too: an interruption leaves the partition half-detached,
still in
.Va pg_inherits
and still reporting
.Va relispartition ,
with only
.Va inhdetachpending
to say so.
Re-running the intent then emits
.Ic FINALIZE ,
which is the only legal move from that state.
On a server older than 14 there is no concurrent form and the plan says the
exclusive lock is unavoidable.
.Ss Unique constraints and primary keys
Both are backed by a unique index, so both are planned through one.
A plain
.Ic ADD CONSTRAINT ... UNIQUE
takes
.Va AccessExclusiveLock
.Em and
.Va ShareLock
and builds the index while holding them.
The two-step route --
.Ic CREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ,
then
.Ic ADD CONSTRAINT ... USING INDEX
-- still takes
.Va AccessExclusiveLock .
That is worth stating precisely, because the usual claim is wrong: it does not
avoid the lock, it avoids the index
.Em build
under the lock.
Same lock level, duration different by orders of magnitude.
On a small table with nothing queued on it the single statement is chosen
instead, since a concurrent build plus a second exclusive lock is more work for
less benefit.
.Pp
.Ic USING INDEX
.Em renames
the backing index to the constraint name, reporting it only as a NOTICE.
.Nm
therefore names the index after the constraint from the start, so the rename is
a no-op; when it adopts an index that already exists under another name, it
says so, because an index named in a dashboard or an alert silently becoming a
different one is exactly the kind of change nobody notices until it matters.
.Pp
A primary key carries a trap worth its own paragraph.
.Ic ADD PRIMARY KEY
sets
.Va NOT NULL
itself and verifies it with a full scan under
.Va AccessExclusiveLock :
measured at 75ms on two million rows against 0.6ms when the column is already
.Va NOT NULL ,
and it grows with the table.
So a primary key over a nullable column runs the
.Cm set_not_null
recipe first, which does that same scan under
.Va ShareUpdateExclusiveLock
and does not block the application.
.Pp
There is no
.Ic NOT VALID
form for a unique constraint -- the index build
.Em is
the validation.
A duplicate therefore fails the concurrent build and leaves an
.Va indisvalid
false index, which
.Ic USING INDEX
then refuses, so the job stops rather than creating a constraint nothing
checked.
Clear the leftover with a
.Cm drop_index
intent before retrying.
.Ss Adding a column is the opposite case
.Cm add_column
is never blocked by a view -- only a type change and a drop are -- so nothing
is rebuilt for it.
What is worth knowing is the reverse: the new column reaches
.Em none
of the existing views, including any written
.Ic SELECT * .
A view stores its column list when it is created and the star is expanded then
and there, so a later
.Ic ADD COLUMN
never arrives.
Nothing errors; the column is simply absent for anything reading through the
view, which is why the plan says so rather than leaving it to be discovered.
.Pp
.Cm replace_view
is how a column is exposed, and it takes the view's
.Em query
rather than a
.Ic CREATE
statement, because choosing the mechanism is the only decision it makes.
For a plain view that is
.Ic CREATE OR REPLACE ,
which costs one loss instead of eight: the comment, per-column comments,
grants, column-level grants,
.Ic INSTEAD OF
triggers, the owner and every dependent object all survive.
The single casualty is the view's options -- the
.Ic WITH
clause replaces the list wholesale, so a view silently stops being a
.Va security_barrier
with no error at all -- and the plan re-applies them in the same transaction.
.Pp
.Ic CREATE OR REPLACE
can only
.Em append
columns; reordering, removing, retyping or renaming one is refused.
A materialized view has no replace form whatsoever, so it is dropped and
recreated
.Ic WITH DATA ,
re-running its query in full while the lock is held.
.Pp
Which type changes rewrite the table is measured, not assumed.
Relaxing or dropping a type modifier is free; adding or tightening one rewrites,
because every existing value must be checked.
.Nm
classifies only the measured cases --
.Li varchar(n)
to a wider
.Li varchar
or to
.Li text ,
.Li numeric(p,s)
to a wider precision at the same scale, and a widened timestamp precision --
and assumes a rewrite otherwise, because reporting one that does not happen
costs a cautious plan while missing one is an unplanned outage under
.Va AccessExclusiveLock .
.Sh PREREQUISITES
Some migrations need something done that this program cannot do, often on a
machine it is not connected to: a replication slot on the publisher, a package
on the database host, free space in a destination tablespace, a credential in
.Pa pg_service.conf .
.Pp
Those are reported as
.Va prerequisites
on the plan, separately from warnings, and structured rather than written out:
each carries a
.Va kind ,
a
.Va target ,
.Va where
it must be done, the
.Va requirement
itself, a
.Va verify
query or command, and whether it is
.Va blocking .
A warning tells a person something; a prerequisite tells an agent what must be
true, on which machine, and how to find out whether it already is.
That difference is what lets a skill act on one -- provision the file, open the
ticket, send the mail -- and then check.
.Pp
An ordinary migration has none.
They are meant to be rare enough to be worth reading.
.Ss Why a credential is never generated
It would be convenient for
.Nm
to invent a password and hand it back.
It will not, for three reasons that are worth stating because the idea is a
reasonable one.
.Pp
The direction is wrong: a subscription connects
.Em to
the publisher, so the password must already exist for that role
.Em there ,
and this program is connected to the subscriber.
A generated one produces a subscription that cannot connect and looks
configured.
.Pp
It would still reach the ledger.
Every statement is stored verbatim in
.Va laswell.step.sql ,
so a generated password would sit in the ledger exactly as an author's would --
the leak moved rather than removed.
.Pp
And it would break determinism.
The same specification against the same observations must produce byte-identical
SQL; that is what makes
.Cm planMigration
a promise about what
.Cm startMigration
will run.
A fresh random value each time destroys it.
.Pp
So the credential stays outside: the specification names a service, the secret
lives in
.Pa pg_service.conf
or
.Pa ~/.pgpass ,
and the prerequisite says what must be there and how to check it.
.Sh CAPACITY
The migrations run inside PostgreSQL, not on the machine
.Nm
runs on: this process contributes three connections and a poll loop per job.
So the client's core count is very nearly irrelevant to how many migrations can
run at once, and the readings below are all server-side.
.Pp
What actually limits concurrency is parallel worker slots, and they run out
long before connections do.
Measured on a stock 18.6,
.Va max_parallel_workers
is 8 and
.Va max_parallel_maintenance_workers
is 2 -- so thirty-two concurrent migrations each wanting a concurrent index
build share eight slots, and past them a build does not queue.
It falls back to single-threaded,
.Em silently ,
and every duration estimated from a measured build is then wrong with nothing
reporting it.
That is what this reading exists to prevent.
.Pp
.Cm planMigration
and
.Cm jobStatus
report, under
.Va budget.workers :
the worker limits, how many parallel workers are in use
.Em right now
-- the only live reading among them -- the free connection headroom, and a
verdict naming the number that decided it, such as
.Qq yes, but all 8 parallel worker slots are in use .
An agent deciding whether to start another job should read that rather than a
core count.
.Pp
.Va host_vcpus
is
.Em configuration ,
never a measurement, and is reported as such.
PostgreSQL exposes no CPU count in SQL at all -- the only cpu-named settings
are planner cost constants -- so it is declared by an operator or it is absent.
.Ss maintenance_work_mem
Raising it is the cheapest speed-up available for an index build, a foreign-key
validation or a table rewrite, and
.Nm
sets it per step for exactly those.
Two facts about it were checked in the documentation rather than assumed,
because the obvious guess is wrong in both directions.
.Pp
It is
.Em not
per worker:
.Qq parallel utility commands treat the resource limit maintenance_work_mem as
.Qq a limit to be applied to the entire utility command, regardless of the
.Qq number of parallel worker processes .
A parallel build does not multiply it.
.Pp
It
.Em is
per concurrent operation, and the documentation's own justification for setting
it high is the assumption concurrent migrations void:
.Qq an installation normally doesn't have many of them running concurrently .
Thirty-two jobs is precisely that.
So
.Va maintenance_work_mem_mb
is a ceiling and the value applied per step is that ceiling divided by
.Va max_concurrent_jobs ,
with the division shown in the plan.
Leave it unset and the server's own setting is used: the host's free memory is
not visible from SQL, and this tool will not guess at the one resource whose
exhaustion is not graceful.
.Sh SIGNING
.Nm
verifies; it never signs.
Obtain the bytes, sign them elsewhere, attach the signature:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pg_laswell_mcp --call getSpecDigest --args @args.json \e
  | jq -r .canonicalBytes > canonical.bin

openssl pkeyutl -sign -inkey key.pem -rawin -in canonical.bin \e
  | base64 -w0
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm getSpecDigest
needs no database and none has to be configured to run it, which is what makes
the step above possible on the machine that holds the private key -- deliberately
not the machine that can reach production.
Every other operation does need a connection, and says so rather than printing
usage.
.Pp
Then add to the specification:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"signatures": [
  { "key_id": "ed25519:<16 hex>",
    "algorithm": "ed25519",
    "signature": "<base64>" }
]
.Ed
.Pp
A key id is the content address of the key, being
.Ql ed25519:
followed by the first sixteen hex characters of the SHA-256 of the raw
thirty-two byte public key.
It therefore cannot be reassigned to different bytes.
.Ss What signing protects
It stops a tampered specification, a mis-signed one, a wrong-environment
mistake, and a permissive or stale client configuration.
.Pp
It does not stop anyone with superuser on the target, who can insert into
.Va laswell.trusted_key
or bypass this program entirely.
Signing answers
.Qq did this exact change come from someone we trust ,
not
.Qq should this change be allowed .
The second question is
.Ic GRANT Ns 's
job.
.Sh TRUST
Two gates, and a specification must pass both.
.Pp
The client configuration is the first: it fails fast and offline, before a
connection is opened.
An absent
.Bq trust
section means there is no local policy to check, which is not the same as
accepting nothing.
.Pp
.Va laswell.trusted_key
in the target database is the second, and the one that decides.
It survives a wrong, stale or permissive local configuration, it verifies
against the key bytes
.Em the database holds
rather than the caller's copy, and
.Va laswell.migration.signer_key_id
is a foreign key to it, so an untrusted signer cannot create a ledger row at
all.
That is a constraint rather than a code path: there is no branch to forget.
.Sh BOOTSTRAP
.Pa sql/bootstrap.sql
is run once, by hand, by a superuser or owner role.
.Nm
never runs it.
.Pp
The reason is a privilege argument, not a convenience one.
If this program created
.Va laswell.trusted_key ,
its role would own it, and a role that owns a table can insert into it, which
means the migrating role could grant itself trust and the gate would collapse
into a comment.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \e
     -v laswell_role=laswell_runner \e
     -v first_key_id=ed25519:9f2c41b7d0e6a85c \e
     -v first_key_b64=... \e
     -v first_key_label=ops-prod \e
     -f bootstrap.sql
.Ed
.Sh CONFIGURATION
Resolved in this order, first match winning:
.Fl -config ,
.Ev PGLASWELL_CONFIG ,
.Pa ~/.config/pg_laswell/laswell.ini
if it exists,
.Ev DATABASE_URL ,
then the first non-option argument.
.Pp
The file must not be group- or world-accessible.
It may hold a password, as any migration tool's configuration may, and it
declares which signing keys are trusted; the same rule as
.Pa ~/.pgpass
covers both.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[trust]
accept = ed25519:9f2c41b7d0e6a85c

[key ed25519:9f2c41b7d0e6a85c]
label      = ops-prod
public_key = MCowBQYDK2VwAyEA...

[executor]
batch_rows          = 1000
commit_interval_ms  = 1000
batch_cap_rows      = 10000
lock_timeout_ms     = 3000
observer_tick_ms    = 250
max_concurrent_jobs = 2
throttle_waiters    = 4
pause_waiters       = 8
resume_waiters      = 1
app_pool_size       = 0

[shop_prod]
service  = shop-prod
dbname   = shop
user     = laswell_runner
password = ...
.Ed
.Pp
Nothing is silently defaulted.
A key that does not parse, an unknown key, or a trailing comma in a list is an
error naming the file, the line and the key.
Settings are checked for consistency too: a
.Va resume_waiters
at or above
.Va pause_waiters
would make the breaker flap, and is refused when the file is read rather than
discovered during a migration.
.Ss The connection budget
.Va app_pool_size
is the application's own connection-pool limit.
It defaults to zero, meaning not configured, and the difference matters: the
ceiling that decides whether an application survives being blocked is its own
pool, not
.Va max_connections ,
because it collapses when its pool fills.
That limit is invisible from the server, so when it is not configured the
budget reports the server's ceiling and says that is what it is reporting.
.Sh THE LEDGER
Schema
.Va laswell ,
created by the bootstrap script, with a
.Ic COMMENT ON
on every table and column so a stranger can read it during an incident.
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Va trusted_key
signing keys this database accepts
.It Va migration
every specification seen, with the exact bytes that were verified
.It Va job
one attempt to apply one migration
.It Va step
what each step did, with the statement verbatim and the rule that chose it
.It Va backfill_cursor
resume position, written by the worker inside the same transaction as the data
it describes
.El
.Pp
A job with no
.Va finished_at
whose advisory lock is absent from
.Va pg_locks
died with its connection.
A session advisory lock is released the moment its backend goes away, so a
crashed job is detectable with no heartbeat and no timeout to tune.
.Ss Operating without an agent
The ledger is the interface for someone who has only psql.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
-- what ran, and why that method
SELECT ordinal, kind, state, why, sql FROM laswell.step
 WHERE job_id = '...';

-- find a live job and its backend
SELECT pid, application_name, query FROM pg_stat_activity
 WHERE application_name LIKE 'pg_laswell/%';

-- stop it; the cursor is committed, so it resumes
SELECT pg_cancel_backend(<pid>);
.Ed
.Sh POOLERS
The executor requires a
.Em direct
connection.
This is a departure from
.Xr pg_licht_mcp 1 ,
which is deliberately pooler-safe, and the reason is measured rather than
stylistic.
.Pp
With
.Va pool_mode = transaction ,
a session GUC set by one client was readable by a
.Em different
client, and an advisory lock taken by one client was visible as held to
another.
PgBouncer does not run
.Va server_reset_query
in transaction mode unless
.Va server_reset_query_always
is enabled, and it is off by default.
Most operators do not know this.
.Pp
Job coordination rests on session advisory locks, and
.Fn pg_try_advisory_lock
is re-entrant within a single session.
Two jobs sharing one server connection would therefore both believe they hold
the exclusive lock on the same table, with no error reported anywhere.
.Pp
Detection at startup is not achievable: comparing
.Fn pg_backend_pid
across two transactions has false negatives at both ends of the pool-size
range.
Instead the worker re-reads its backend PID at the start of every transaction
and aborts the job on any change, which fires at the moment the connection is
actually multiplexed.
A negative result is never reported as a direct connection having been
confirmed.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev DATABASE_URL
libpq connection string, used when no registry file is given.
.It Ev PGLASWELL_CONFIG
Path to the connection registry.
.It Ev PGLASWELL_REQUIRE_DATABASE
Test-suite only: turns a skipped database test into a failure.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Without
.Fl -call ,
.Nm
speaks MCP over stdio and exits 0 unless it could not start.
.Pp
With
.Fl -call
it exits 0 when the tool answered successfully and 1 otherwise, so a pipeline
can gate on it.
The MCP notion of an error is narrower than a build's: a plan that correctly
refuses is a successful call and an unsuccessful outcome, and a build must fail
on the second.
Exit 1 therefore covers a thrown tool, an
.Va error
payload, a refused plan
.Pq Ql ok: false ,
an unaccepted specification
.Pq Ql accepted: false ,
and a repository with a non-empty
.Va problems
array, which is the case that makes
.Ic listMigrations
usable as a build gate.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pg_laswell_mcp --call listMigrations \e
  --args '{"directory":"db/migrations"}' || exit 1
.Ed
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check what a role can do before trusting an answer from it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pg_laswell_mcp --call checkPrivileges
.Ed
.Pp
Plan without applying, and read the reasoning:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pg_laswell_mcp --call planMigration --args @spec-args.json \e
  | jq -r .rendered
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr pg_licht_mcp 1 ,
.Xr psql 1
.Sh STANDARDS
Model Context Protocol, revisions 2024-11-05 through 2025-11-25, over stdio
using JSON-RPC 2.0.
Specification canonicalisation follows RFC 8785.
Signatures are Ed25519, RFC 8032.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Daniel Cristian Cruz
.Sh CAVEATS
This is pre-release software.
The specification format, the ledger schema and the tool surface are all
expected to change before 1.0.
.Pp
PostgreSQL 16 or newer is needed for the relation derivation that
.Ic listMigrations
uses for concurrency advice; on older servers it says so and reports less.
